course: dictionary
lesson: 11569
title: A word that is not English
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Ang» is a fragment, a small piece of sound or writing that is not an ordinary English word. You will meet it in three places: in Mandarin Chinese, in Filipino (Tagalog), and as a surname. What it means depends on where you find it.
explain: In Mandarin it is a sound, like «áng» (昂, to raise the head). In Tagalog, «ang» means «the», as in «Ang aso ay malaki», which means "The dog is big." In a name, Ang is a surname, like the film director Ang Lee.
explain: Context clues help here too. If the words around «ang» are Chinese or Filipino, it means what that language says. If it comes after a first name, it is probably a surname.
fact: «Ang» is not an English word, so you will not find it in an ordinary English sentence.
fact: In Tagalog, «ang» means «the»: «Ang bata» means "the child".
fact: In Mandarin, «áng» (昂) means to raise the head, and it sounds like «ahng» to an English ear.
fact: As a surname, Ang is found in Southeast Asia, like the film director «Ang Lee».
fact: Look at the words around it. Chinese or Filipino words nearby show you which meaning you have.
exercise: In Tagalog, «Ang aso ay malaki» means "The dog is big." What does «ang» mean here? | *the | dog | a | big
exercise: Is «ang» an ordinary English word? | Yes, it is a name for a cat | Yes, it means happy | Yes, it means the | *No, it is a fragment from another language
exercise: In «Ang Lee made a famous film», what kind of word is Ang? | *A surname, a family name | An English verb | A sound that means raise your head | A Tagalog word for the
test: In «Ang bata ay masaya», which means "The child is happy", what does «ang» mean? | happy | child | *the | a
test: What kind of word is Ang in a name like «Ang Lee»? | an English verb | *a surname | a sound in Mandarin | a Tagalog word for the
exam: Which statement about «ang» is true? | It is a number | It always means the, in every language | It is an ordinary English word that means the | *It is a fragment that means different things in Mandarin, Tagalog, and as a surname
